 @Tenchigumi  Frame generation is imperfect and does cause unwanted artifacts. I use it to get emulated games from 30fps to 60fps, but it causes screen tearing and ghosting.

Same thing (but even worse) happened to me when using Frame Generation on the Monster Hunter Wilds Demo, the experience was terrible and it only improved when I turned it off. Playing at lower FPS count by disabling frame generation made the overall experience much better.

I doubt you're doing anything wrong, it's just the limits of this technique.

 @CyborgNinjaJesus  The cooler is really impressive but from a building standpoint and from the fact alone, a small cooler like that is able to handle such load. However, that doesn't mean it runs insanely cool which it does not. 
I personally couldn't care less of how impressive something is from a building standpoint and the level of detail when temps in the end are just high regardless. 

If a car manufacturer build a car with only 2hp that could drive 60mph an hour, that certainly would be impressive but it's still slow. I think you get my point and how insanely irrelevant it is how impressive the cooler really is. It does not run cool.

 @anakinlowground5515  Funny you say that because game studios have been using similar technology already. 
Asynchronous Reprojection, Interpolation, Extrapolation etc. 

You people need to stop calling something fake just for the sake of it. Yes, DLSS  has drawbacks but deciding between CP2077 completely maxed out in 4k and full RT at 27fps compared to DLSS with FG at over 200fps is pretty much a no brainer decision IF input lag doesn't bother you which is absolutely fair to keep in mind. Sit there and cry about the frames being fake. If input lag is fine, the tech is amazing. Those fames are not fake. They are generated based on AI algorymths and in the end therefore exist. No one would argue they are natural rendered frames and come with no drawbacks but you act like they don't exist which is just wrong since you literally see them lol.

@apersonontheinternet8006  I completely disagree. I just upgraded from a 2070 super because it couldn't even handle COD at 1440p without graphical bugs and stuttering from low VRAM. 1080p has no purpose in modern hardware. The consoles can easily hit 60fps at 1440p for $500. Why in the hell would you buy a $600 GPU to play at 1080p? Hell we're at the point most GPUs get zero frame rate benefit from 1080p as we long hit CPU bottlenecks. 

And DLSS quality 1080p doesn't look anywhere close to 1440p native to me. DLSS already causes smearing in motion from the temporal nature, and it also causes ghosting and small objects to flicker or straight up disappear like snow or confetti. DLSS looks worse the lower res you go.

And if you're seriously still using a 1080p display, that should be what you're upgrading. Displays in the last 10 years have gone miles in color, brightness, motion clarity, and even full aray dimming. You can get a full aray backlit 4K display, for $350. Why would you stick with a 10 year old 1080p display instead? Like 1080p display aren't even good value. Like the good ones are still nearly $100 and you can get decent 1440p ones with Quantum dot for $200-250. And if a GPU can handle 60fps at 4K, that's nearly 240hz at 1080p, I highly doubt that 1080p display most people have are.

