@SWOTHDRAyes, let’s. 

It’s up to game developers to actually optimize their games, if it doesn’t run on the hardware available that’s not the hardwares problem. (Not every game is crysis, trying to futureproof their graphics for the next era, and not every game SHOULD) 

Shortcuts by offloading to proprietary ray tracing cores is bad. Unoptimized nanite is bad. Lighting and textures that costs sometimes 3x what they should and could, with zero noticeable difference when implemented is a show that it’s bad. Game companies are giving these devs less and less time to actually tune their engines and so they suck.

@zeemya Depends on your definition of playable I suppose. My personal definition is at least 1080p, high settings, 60 fps without DLSS and without ray tracing. By those metrics the 1080 Ti is still playable on almost everything. It having 11gb of gDDR5X really kept it relevant a long time.

I have my old 1080Ti on my living room PC with a 4K TV and honestly it still handles damn near everything I throw at it now like 7 years later. Indiana Jones and GoW: Ragnarok are about the only things I have to crank back on.

Oh and the Shad PS4 emulator with Bloodborne, that one is still pretty hit or miss.

